00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.950
So let's get started.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.430
So Cloud Spanner--
we've talked about it

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.170
in a few other webinars as well.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:40.410
But today, we wanted to take
a deeper dive into how we get

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:42.230
eventual-- sorry--

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:46.670
global external consistency
with this system

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.030
with asset transactions
and good performance.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.840
So let's do a little
recap, in case you're

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.940
joining us for the first time,
of what Cloud Spanner is.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.660
So here is a suite of our
managed database and storage

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.300
services on Google
Cloud Platform.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.240
So as you can see,
we have a wide array

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:11.740
of services to meet all
needs of your applications

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.330
from the storage and
database standpoint.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:18.370
We have Cloud Storage, which
is our binary object store.

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:22.510
We have our key values stored
in Memcache App Engine.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.560
And then we have a data
store and Cloud Bigtable,

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.349
which is our
non-relational offerings.

00:01:30.349 --> 00:01:32.640
And on the relational side,
we have Cloud SQL and Cloud

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.980
Spanner, which is our scale-out,
our relational database

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.090
service.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:39.880
And on the other end,
we have BigQuery,

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.800
which is our enterprise
data warehouse.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.620
So with Cloud Spanner
and Cloud BigQuery,

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.180
we have the full suite of
scale-out relational database

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.410
as well as scale-out
enterprise data warehouse.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:56.680
So what is Cloud
Spanner, really?

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.960
So it's the only
enterprise-grade,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:00.490
globally distributed,
and strongly

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:04.600
consistent managed database
service built for the cloud

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.080
specifically to combine the
benefits of relational database

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.190
structure and non-relational
horizontal scale.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:15.300
What does that mean?

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.600
So basically, with
Cloud Spanner,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.070
you don't have to
make tradeoffs.

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:23.480
You can get schemas, asset
transactions across rows,

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.100
regions, and continents, along
with SQL querying, so not

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:31.610
something that sort of
resembles SQL, but actual SQL

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:35.690
with ANSI 2011 SQL
compliance query engine.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:39.750
And you get the benefits
of a horizontally scalable

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:40.640
no-SQL system.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.787
So we offer up to five
nines of availability,

00:02:43.787 --> 00:02:44.870
and that's a lot of nines.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.430
But what that really
means is we guarantee

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:47.846
less than five
minutes of downtime

00:02:47.846 --> 00:02:52.430
a year, which is
industry-leading for the SLA.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:55.950
It is a fully managed and
easily scalable service.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.830
So what that means is you
can-- with a few clicks,

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:03.200
you can deploy a globe-spanning
database, and be ready to go.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:11.320
So let's get into how
we are able to achieve

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:16.010
this no-tradeoff
industry leading system.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:19.160
So what is the foundation
on which we're established?

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.150
So first of all, as
you can see here,

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.330
Google Cloud
Platform has a number

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:25.580
of regions and more coming.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:29.660
In fact, we added something
like 39 zones last year alone,

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:32.510
and we continue to
invest in the platform

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:36.290
and in the infrastructure
for our customers.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:40.420
So as I said, we
added 39 new zones,

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.880
and we continue to add more.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.080
And as you can see, we
have data centers and zones

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.290
across the world
to provide services

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:54.010
close to where the customers are
and where the applications need

00:03:54.010 --> 00:04:01.390
to be for our customers to
provide the best application

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:04.120
performance to their end users.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:11.320
Now on this platform, we have
built Cloud Spanner instances.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.920
So we have instances
in two flavors.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.810
There are regional
Cloud Spanner instances

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.519
and multi-region instances.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.920
So in this case, with
regional Cloud Spanner,

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.410
you can see we're available
in multiple regions on GCP

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.820
across the world, and we're
adding more regions as we go.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:31.740
In fact, our aim
is to basically be

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:35.480
part of the initial footprint
in all regions moving forward

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.360
as we launch more
regions on GCP.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.410
What this means is that we
have replicated instant--

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.090
like basically, we
have three zones

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.040
that are replicating
inside each region.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.757
So if you have data locality or
data governance concerns, then

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:53.340
with Cloud Spanner
regional instances,

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.500
you can put your entire
database in one region,

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.390
and the data remains
in that region.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:01.180
The other flavor
as I talked about

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.360
is multi-region configurations.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:07.090
And the first one we
are talking about here

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.830
is a continental multi-region.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:12.190
So as you can see, there
are two data centers,

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:15.850
where the Cloud Spanner
configuration exists,

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:18.730
which means there are five
zones across those two data

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:23.260
centers that support
the databases you

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:24.290
create in that instance.

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:30.020
Then there is a multi-region
global instance,

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.910
which is North America,
Europe, and Asia.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.780
So here, what's important
to note is that when we look

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:39.200
at the green bounding
box for both the regional

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.600
and the multi-region--

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.570
sorry, the regional single
continent and multi-continent

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:47.510
configurations, that
you're really talking

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:49.820
to one Cloud Spanner database.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:53.516
And that one database is being
replicated across the zones

00:05:53.516 --> 00:05:54.890
and regions we
talked about here,

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.180
but that's transparent to you.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:00.410
So in this case,
we have replicas

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:04.220
across nine zones
in the four regions

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.570
that are marked here for
the North America, Europe,

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:08.420
and Asia configuration.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:13.130
And for you, you talk to one
Cloud Spanner database that

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:16.370
is transparently replicated
across those nine zones,

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:18.530
but you never have to
choose which zone to go to

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:19.700
or which replica to talk to.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:21.533
That's something that
the API takes care of.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:29.620
Now, that's about like the
physical location of the data,

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:33.100
but how is that data connected?

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:35.860
How are those data
centers that GCP has,

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:39.410
how are their zones
and regions connected?

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:41.650
So as you know, or
you may have heard of,

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:44.690
Google Cloud has
its own network.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:48.700
In fact, Google built its own
networking fabric a while ago

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:50.650
and continues to
invest in it, which

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.650
means that we connect
our data centers,

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:57.310
and we want to put
requests and user traffic

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.710
on our network as quickly
as possible, which

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:02.320
is different from
other cloud providers,

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.852
because usually, they have
to deal with the hot potato

00:07:05.852 --> 00:07:07.810
problem, where if their
network gets congested,

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:10.000
they offload to the
public internet,

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.640
whereas in Google's
case, we actually

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.840
want to put your requests on our
network as quickly as possible,

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:20.390
because we have built the
network to be redundant.

00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:25.130
We have multiple links
to the same place,

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:27.850
so multiple routes to the
same two connection points.

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:31.600
And we've built the stack up
to be resilient and highly

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:33.460
performant, so we
can predict latencies

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:36.350
across these
different endpoints.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:39.190
And therefore, we can
give better performance,

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:42.190
and we don't have to rely
on the routing constraints

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:45.520
and latencies of the public
internet in most cases.

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:50.000
And as I was talking
about earlier,

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:51.610
let's go into the
hot potato problem.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.090
So basically, what a
typical cloud provider does

00:07:54.090 --> 00:08:00.150
is they try to offload user
traffic to the public internet

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:03.030
as quickly as possible,
because they don't have

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:04.440
as much bandwidth provision.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:07.470
So in most cases, the
network gets congested,

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:10.720
especially as they're new--

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:12.706
especially if there's
higher traffic

00:08:12.706 --> 00:08:13.830
volumes that are happening.

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:15.450
So as soon as that
happens, a lot

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:18.180
of people are asking for the
same data across same data

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:18.990
points--

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:20.110
end points.

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:23.790
Then the traffic gets offloaded
to the public internet, which

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:26.490
has its own congestion
problems and its own sort

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:28.150
of traffic issues.

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:30.360
So there isn't predictable
performance and latency

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.610
in that case.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:34.539
But with Google Cloud and
with Google in general,

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:37.650
we try to put user
traffic as quickly as

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:42.520
possible on the Google
network so that, as I said,

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:45.930
we have built the stack very
carefully so that we can

00:08:45.930 --> 00:08:49.330
predict performance for
the network in this case,

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:51.870
and therefore, can
offer good performance

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:57.150
and predictable latencies to
the customers of Google Cloud.

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.970
Now, let's get to the specifics
of Cloud Spanner itself.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:02.989
So there are multiple concepts
that you may have come across

00:09:02.989 --> 00:09:03.780
with Cloud Spanner.

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:07.020
There is nodes,
replicas, and the data

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:09.420
that lives in Cloud Spanner.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:15.390
So with Cloud Spanner, you buy
nodes, and you get storage.

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:18.150
And they scale independently,
so you can keep

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.019
adding data to Cloud Spanner.

00:09:20.019 --> 00:09:21.810
You can keep writing
data to your database,

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:24.180
and the storage
will automatically

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:25.920
scale to absorb that data.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:30.150
And the compute
for that database

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:32.400
is coming from the nodes
that you provision.

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.024
That's something you
have to provision,

00:09:34.024 --> 00:09:37.690
and it's a few clicks that you
go to the UI, to our console,

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:40.080
and then just add nodes,
and within a few seconds

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:43.410
to a minute or a few minutes,
you get the additional nodes.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.360
And then the data is
automatically sharded

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.210
across those new
nodes, which means

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:50.574
you won't have to take care
of physical provisioning,

00:09:50.574 --> 00:09:51.990
obviously, but you
also don't have

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:53.850
to take care of the data
sharding and rebalancing.

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:55.933
That's something that Cloud
Spanner takes care of.

00:09:55.933 --> 00:09:58.570
The system takes care of itself.

00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:02.070
So let's get into the concepts
of how these things work

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:05.710
and differ from each other.

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:09.480
So let's talk about the
regional configuration first.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.210
So as I mentioned
before, Cloud Spanner

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:16.650
is replicated across multiple
zones either within a region

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:20.100
or in the multi-region
case across multiple zones

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:22.380
in multiple GCP regions.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:23.970
So in the regional
configuration,

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:29.080
we have three replicas that are
in three different availability

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:31.544
zones within a
single GCP region.

00:10:31.544 --> 00:10:33.210
So in this case, we're
looking at, let's

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:36.930
say, European region 1,
which has availability zones

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:40.927
A, B, and C within which a Cloud
Spanner instance will exist.

00:10:40.927 --> 00:10:43.260
So when you go to the console
and create a Cloud Spanner

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:45.930
instance, and you choose
a regional configuration,

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:47.760
any regional
configuration, it will

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:50.430
come with three
availability zones

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:54.870
within which your
instance will exist.

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:58.560
And all the data and
all the databases

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.240
that you create
within that instance

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:04.240
will be replicated in
this configuration.

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:07.850
And as you can see here, there
are three read-write replicas

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:11.250
in the three availability zones.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:14.810
Now, going to a more
interesting picture,

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:16.640
let's look at our
global configuration,

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:20.540
which is our NAM-EUR-ASIA-1
configuration that we looked

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:22.162
at earlier.

00:11:22.162 --> 00:11:23.870
In this case, as I
had mentioned earlier,

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:28.430
there are nine
replicas across three

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:33.390
different continents and nine
different availability zones.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.980
So in this particular
case, if you notice,

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:39.800
the right quorum
has five replicas

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:45.240
that are all in the US, in
North America in this case.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:49.010
And there are two replicas
each in Asia and Europe

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:50.926
for read-only workloads.

00:11:50.926 --> 00:11:53.300
So the way to think about the
multi-region configurations

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:57.320
is that you're trading off
write latency, a little bit

00:11:57.320 --> 00:11:59.540
higher write latency
for very low read

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:04.010
latency close to your
clients and users.

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:06.620
So data from Cloud
Spanner is always

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:09.440
going to be served from the
closest, healthiest replica

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:14.510
that it can satisfy the
request that the user has made.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:18.140
And in this case, yes, all
write requests go to the US,

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:19.370
and we'll get into that.

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:22.280
But because we have
our network that I

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:24.770
talked about that has
predictable low latencies

00:12:24.770 --> 00:12:26.900
and is redundant and
has multiple routes

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:30.110
to two different
endpoints, because of that,

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:34.700
we can actually offer pretty low
latency and good performance,

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:38.690
even for the writes going
all the way to the US.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:42.600
So digging one layer
deeper, let's, again,

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.570
look at the regional
configuration first.

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:48.090
When you create a Cloud
Spanner instance, as I said,

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:50.280
you get three replicas.

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:53.160
Transparently, you never know
that there are three replicas

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:54.450
in your instance.

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:57.300
You could choose a
regional instance

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:59.790
or a multi-region configuration
for your instance,

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:01.430
and the number of
replicas and all

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:04.260
of that, essentially,
implementation detail will

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:05.190
be hidden from you.

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:06.780
What you do is
talk to an instance

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:08.310
and a database
within that instance.

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:10.143
And as I mentioned
earlier, you can actually

00:13:10.143 --> 00:13:13.270
create multiple databases
within a Cloud Spanner instance,

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:17.200
if you need the same
replication topology for them.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:20.730
So in this case, we have two
databases within the Cloud

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:22.230
Spanner instance
that are replicated

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:25.740
across the three zones that
exist for this Cloud Spanner

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:26.900
configuration.

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:33.850
So let's look at the
next layer of how writes

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:37.130
and updates work in
this configuration.

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:39.910
So when an update comes
in, the system knows--

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:42.730
so first of all, you're, again,
talking to one Cloud Spanner

00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:46.090
database within a configuration,
so you're basically

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:48.860
exposed to one Cloud Spanner
endpoint for the database.

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:51.510
You don't ever have
to think about or code

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:55.620
in your application which
replica you should go to.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.457
That's not a decision
that you have to make.

00:13:57.457 --> 00:13:59.290
That's something that
the system and the API

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:02.170
will take care of, which
means that in this case,

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:05.130
if an update comes in
for Table 1 and Table 2,

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.030
so if you want to
update key 1 and key 2

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:10.360
in Table 1 and Table
2, then the API

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:13.930
figures out where the
leader of these keys

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:17.960
is and will send the
request to that leader.

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:24.850
So we use Paxos as the consensus
mechanism for our write

00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:27.460
commits, so there's
more information

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:28.720
on Paxos on the link.

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:30.940
But basically, it's
a consensus protocol

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:35.200
that says that as long as
a majority of replicas,

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:36.504
essentially, agree--

00:14:36.504 --> 00:14:38.170
as long as the majority
agrees, then you

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:43.044
can commit the transaction
or commit data.

00:14:43.044 --> 00:14:45.210
So in this case, this is
why we have three replicas,

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:47.001
so as long as two out
of the three replicas

00:14:47.001 --> 00:14:51.340
agree on any change, then
that change can be persisted.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:53.810
So in this case, going
back to our example,

00:14:53.810 --> 00:14:57.650
if you wanted to update Table
1 and Table 2 as in key 1

00:14:57.650 --> 00:15:00.130
and key 2 in Table
1 and Table 2,

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:04.960
the protocol will route
the message, the request

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:08.630
to the leader of the replica--

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:11.680
sorry, leader of these keys
need a replica for these keys.

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:15.400
And then that leader is
responsible for sending

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:18.130
the request out to
the other two replicas

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:21.490
and getting an acknowledgment
back and committing the data.

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:24.910
And by the time you get
an acknowledgment for that

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:27.370
commit back, the
data has already

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:29.410
been-- the mutation has
already been shipped out

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:31.540
to the other two replicas.

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:36.550
So if you do a read after write,
like if you make a read request

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:40.810
as soon as you get an update
commit acknowledgment back,

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:44.900
then you are guaranteed to see
the latest update of the data.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.020
This is the coolness
of Cloud Spanner

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:49.030
and its global
external consistency,

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:53.820
that you can actually do a
read after a committed app,

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:56.830
and you're guaranteed
to see the latest data.

00:15:56.830 --> 00:15:58.600
And you don't have
to ever think about

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:00.482
whether the data was
eventually consistent,

00:16:00.482 --> 00:16:02.690
which means that if you
asked two different replicas,

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:04.148
will you get two
different answers?

00:16:04.148 --> 00:16:06.280
In fact, you can ask for
the data from anywhere

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:09.470
in the world, and you will
get the same answer back

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:11.200
guaranteeing the
external consistency

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:13.910
that we talked about.

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:16.500
Now, let's get into the
more interesting example

00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:19.080
of the multi-region
configurations and the reads

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:20.550
and write requests there.

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:22.770
So starting with
the write request,

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:28.440
if there's an update to be
made from a US client, then

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:31.230
basically, the client can just--

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:35.130
the API will route the
client request for the write

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.740
to the read-write quorum,
which is in the US,

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:40.590
and then the request
response will be routed back

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:42.450
to the client from the US.

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:46.170
So there's a round-trip between
the US client and the US quorum

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:48.030
and back.

00:16:48.030 --> 00:16:49.770
In case there is
a client in Asia--

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:54.690
and again, if you remember,
we have the read-write quorum

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:58.380
in the US with five replicas
across three different data

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:01.860
centers, and then we
have read-only replicas

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:03.670
in Asia and in Europe.

00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:09.210
So if you have a client in Asia
making a read-write request

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:12.910
as in an update request
for the database,

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:17.880
basically, we'll try and put the
request on the Google network,

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:19.740
on a Google front end
as quickly as possible

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:21.480
as close to the Asia
client as possible.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:25.230
So within Asia, we'll basically
put the request on our network,

00:17:25.230 --> 00:17:29.480
and from there, it'll be routed
to the read-write client--

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:34.040
sorry, the read-write
quorum in the US.

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:38.320
And the acknowledgment will
come back to the Asia client.

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:40.680
So the writes, as I said,
are routed to the US,

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:44.160
but because we put the
request as quickly as possible

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:48.090
on the Google network,
we have guaranteed

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.790
sort of low latencies.

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:55.980
And we have predictable
performance for these requests.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:58.707
Now, if you make a strong read,
which is a consistent read, so

00:17:58.707 --> 00:18:00.540
basically, if you update
a key, and then you

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:05.970
make a request to read the key
from Asia, again, then because

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:08.670
of the global
external consistency,

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:12.570
we guarantee that you will see
the latest update in this case.

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:15.180
So the important
thing to note here

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:19.807
is that the data is being
served from the local replica.

00:18:19.807 --> 00:18:21.390
The deal is not
actually being shipped

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:24.390
from the US read-write
quorum, which

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:27.840
is an important distinction,
because what we're saying

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:31.860
is that as we commit, as
part of the commit protocol,

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:34.650
the leaders actually ship
out the mutations, not

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:36.480
the data itself,
but the mutations

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:37.740
to all other replicas.

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:40.500
And then the replicas
apply the data themselves,

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:44.370
so it's a fully distributed
system, where each replica acts

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:49.650
independently with
consensus and making sure

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:53.190
that we get the accurate data
and the consistency guarantees.

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:56.190
But each replica is applying
this mutation independently

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:57.869
in its own data structures.

00:18:57.869 --> 00:18:59.910
So what that means is
unlike other systems, where

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:01.500
we have one master
replica, and then

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:04.290
the data is being shipped out
to the other slave replicas,

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:05.910
in this case, all
the replicas behave

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:09.240
as one global
database, which means

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:14.400
that even though the read-write
quorum exists in the US

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:17.700
as part of committing
that write,

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:20.460
the mutations are sent across
to all read-only replicas,

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:24.540
and then they apply the
mutations independently.

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:29.850
And then the data is served
from the closest healthy replica

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:32.010
to the client
making the request.

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:35.700
What that means is in
this case, the data

00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:38.440
is being served from the
read-only replica in Asia,

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:41.040
which means, of course,
it's lower latency and more

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:42.450
predictable performance.

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:45.480
So you don't have to get the
data from the US or from,

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:48.660
essentially, the quorum
in a different continent.

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:52.590
So you're not shipping
huge blocks of data

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:55.639
across oceanic links, which
means it doesn't take as long,

00:19:55.639 --> 00:19:57.930
and therefore, we can have
better performance and lower

00:19:57.930 --> 00:19:58.560
latency.

00:19:58.560 --> 00:19:59.820
That is just one of
the optimizations

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:01.470
we've made to both
the Paxos protocol

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:03.570
and to the entire
Cloud Spanner stack

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:08.040
to be able to perform these
multi-region reads and writes

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:11.102
efficiently for
the applications.

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:16.990
Now, when you're
making a strong read,

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:21.970
there is a ping to the
quorum, which is in the US,

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:25.030
to make sure that the Asia
replica has the latest data

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:29.020
and knows what the last
commit time for that key was.

00:20:29.020 --> 00:20:31.470
So again, you're making a
ping round-trip to the US,

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:33.220
but you're not actually
shipping the data.

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:35.020
The data itself is
served from Asia.

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:43.270
Now, this is another interesting
feature of Cloud Spanner

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:45.330
that we have these
bounded read requests.

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:47.830
So because Cloud Spanner is
a multi-version concurrency

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:50.117
control system, it
means that we actually

00:20:50.117 --> 00:20:51.700
maintain older
versions of the data up

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:54.250
to a certain bounded time.

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:57.945
And if you choose
bounded staleness,

00:20:57.945 --> 00:21:02.530
or if you choose a read request
of a certain staleness of time,

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:05.230
then the chances
of the data being

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:09.380
served from their local
replica are much, much higher.

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:11.320
So you don't even have
to-- because you're not

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:13.070
asking for the latest
data, you don't even

00:21:13.070 --> 00:21:16.330
have to contact the quorum
in a different continent

00:21:16.330 --> 00:21:19.300
to ask for what the last
commit time for this key was.

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:23.890
You can actually just serve
the data from the local replica

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:26.744
without having that
RTT to the leader.

00:21:26.744 --> 00:21:28.660
So this actually leads
to the very low latency

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:32.980
reads from anywhere close to
all of these multiple regions

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:34.480
for the multi-region
configurations.

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:38.680
This is the tradeoff,
which gets even more

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:41.560
acute when you look at
your boundedly stale reads

00:21:41.560 --> 00:21:43.540
and writes.

00:21:43.540 --> 00:21:45.280
So the writes will
go to the US--

00:21:45.280 --> 00:21:46.720
in this case, to the quorum.

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:50.560
But the reads, especially
stale reads or bounded reads,

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:53.560
up to a particular time can be
served from the local replica.

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:57.100
So if you say, I want to
get the value of these keys

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:59.590
up to 15 minutes ago,
up to 15 seconds ago,

00:21:59.590 --> 00:22:02.100
you will get the data locally
from the local replica.

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:10.370
Let's get into another layer,
which is automatic sharding

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:13.371
and life of query and how--

00:22:13.371 --> 00:22:14.870
what we talked about
are high level.

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:18.630
Like how does it actually get
served from Cloud Spanner?

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:20.665
So again, going back
to our regional picture

00:22:20.665 --> 00:22:26.020
of three replicas, here, we have
three replicas with the compute

00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:27.590
and storage highlighted.

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:33.770
So when you provision one
node in Cloud Spanner,

00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:37.140
it's actually one
node per region.

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.960
Sorry, one node per
availability zone.

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:43.060
So this means that in the
regional configuration,

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:46.480
you're actually getting one
node in Zone A, one in Zone B,

00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:48.670
and one in Zone C.
So there are actually

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:52.930
three nodes worth of
compute that you're getting

00:22:52.930 --> 00:22:55.000
across these three zones.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:57.172
And in this configuration
in the picture,

00:22:57.172 --> 00:22:58.630
we actually have
three nodes, which

00:22:58.630 --> 00:23:03.130
is our minimum for
guaranteeing the SLA.

00:23:03.130 --> 00:23:06.040
So in this case, when you
sort of provision three nodes,

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:08.340
you're actually getting
nine nodes total,

00:23:08.340 --> 00:23:09.400
right is hidden from you.

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:12.130
Again, you don't have to worry
about the complexity of how

00:23:12.130 --> 00:23:15.040
these things break down
in terms of provisioning.

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:18.400
You just think of one node and
one node's worth of compute

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:22.400
for your database
and your instance.

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:24.180
So as I said,
storage and compute

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:27.670
are the two axes of
Cloud Spanner that

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:30.040
scale mostly independently.

00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:32.890
In the storage case, we
actually shard the data.

00:23:32.890 --> 00:23:36.910
That's what allows us to
be as scalable as we are.

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:39.250
And the sharding
is both automatic,

00:23:39.250 --> 00:23:43.100
and resharding happens
automatically as well.

00:23:43.100 --> 00:23:45.070
So the data
assignment of a shard,

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:49.000
which here in Cloud Spanner
terminology is a split,

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:51.490
is done by the system.

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:54.850
And assignment of those
shards or splits to the nodes

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:56.890
is also done automatically
by the system.

00:23:56.890 --> 00:24:00.310
So the same shards exist
across all three zones,

00:24:00.310 --> 00:24:02.890
which is where we're
getting consistently

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:05.500
sharding the same way
across three zones.

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:07.180
But the assignment
of those shards

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:13.550
can be different, depending on
the load and the other workload

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:14.730
characteristics.

00:24:14.730 --> 00:24:17.390
Again, this entire thing
is done automatically,

00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:20.180
so you don't ever have to worry
about resharding or rebalancing

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:23.120
your data.

00:24:23.120 --> 00:24:24.820
So in the regional
configuration,

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:27.380
let's look at this split.

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:30.550
So a database has
multiple splits,

00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:32.890
so a database has
multiple tables that

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:34.270
have multiple keys in them.

00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:38.500
And basically, a set of
keys together is a split.

00:24:38.500 --> 00:24:41.500
So we basically are
splitting the database

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:45.850
across a full set
of keys, and each

00:24:45.850 --> 00:24:48.790
split-- as I said, the
splits happen consistently

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:50.070
across the three zones.

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:55.780
So each split within one
zone is the same split

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:56.960
on the other zones as well.

00:24:56.960 --> 00:25:00.230
And together, they
form a Paxos group.

00:25:00.230 --> 00:25:02.770
And so in this illustrative
example, like Split 1

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:06.770
has this one Paxos group
across three zones.

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:08.660
And that is repeated
for all the splits.

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:12.040
Each split, essentially,
forms a group

00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:14.580
along which we run
the Paxos protocol.

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:20.720
There's some optimizations
here, but basic terminology

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:21.230
is the same.

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:22.855
And basically, that's
what we're doing.

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:29.230
So how are these splits done?

00:25:29.230 --> 00:25:34.360
As I was talking about earlier,
basically, we have a key range.

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:37.000
A table has multiple
keys, and the key space

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:40.000
itself is what
we're partitioning.

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:41.170
so a database has tables.

00:25:41.170 --> 00:25:44.230
It has keys, and those
keys have a key range,

00:25:44.230 --> 00:25:47.200
and each key range
forms a split.

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:51.790
So in this case, if we have keys
from 0 to 2000, as you can see,

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:54.370
we have four splits.

00:25:54.370 --> 00:25:56.290
And the splits don't
have to be even.

00:25:56.290 --> 00:25:59.147
They can be in terms
of the number of keys.

00:25:59.147 --> 00:26:00.730
It depends on the
workload, as I said.

00:26:00.730 --> 00:26:05.950
So if certain keys are getting
more work, more queries

00:26:05.950 --> 00:26:07.660
on them, they have
a higher QPS, then

00:26:07.660 --> 00:26:09.760
they will be put on
a separate split,

00:26:09.760 --> 00:26:11.560
because we do both
size-based splitting

00:26:11.560 --> 00:26:12.826
and load-based splitting.

00:26:12.826 --> 00:26:14.200
And again, this
is all automatic,

00:26:14.200 --> 00:26:16.150
so as load increases,
load base splitting

00:26:16.150 --> 00:26:19.300
will split it into
further splits.

00:26:19.300 --> 00:26:21.160
And if there is less
load, then the splits

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:23.500
will merge to form
bigger splits,

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:28.450
because the nodes or the
splits can handle the data--

00:26:28.450 --> 00:26:31.010
sorry, the workload
that's coming in.

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:33.220
So in this case, we
have four splits.

00:26:33.220 --> 00:26:39.040
And each of those splits has
a group with a group leader.

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:40.870
And as you can see,
the split is the same

00:26:40.870 --> 00:26:44.800
in Zone A, Zone B, and Zone C.
So high level, basically, we're

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:48.100
saying that each replica
has the same split of data,

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:51.430
and that split combination
becomes the group.

00:26:51.430 --> 00:26:53.980
And each group has a leader.

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:57.520
And so when strong reads
and writes come in,

00:26:57.520 --> 00:27:01.030
they go to the group leader,
who is then responsible for,

00:27:01.030 --> 00:27:04.000
in the case of writes,
for getting consensus on

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:05.920
whether the write
should commit or not.

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:08.500
And in the case of strong reads,
it's responsible for saying,

00:27:08.500 --> 00:27:11.470
here's the last update
that happened to this split

00:27:11.470 --> 00:27:14.450
or to the specific
key in the split.

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:19.760
So again, just to
recap, we have database

00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:23.570
with a bunch of keys,
which makes the key space.

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:31.040
And the spanner splits up that
key space into multiple splits.

00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:35.630
And the split is
replicated across all zones

00:27:35.630 --> 00:27:38.510
within an instance
configuration.

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:42.765
And each split
group has a leader.

00:27:42.765 --> 00:27:44.390
Now, again, the
important thing to note

00:27:44.390 --> 00:27:46.790
is that leadership
can also change and is

00:27:46.790 --> 00:27:49.620
done automatically
based on health

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:51.740
and a bunch of other
heuristics and metrics

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:53.320
that we're constantly
monitoring.

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:58.280
So for Spanner, changing
leader is not necessarily

00:27:58.280 --> 00:28:00.300
a failure-only scenario.

00:28:00.300 --> 00:28:02.630
It's actually done all the time.

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:05.270
We're constantly
changing the split group

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:09.242
leader, because we're looking
at a bunch of heuristics

00:28:09.242 --> 00:28:10.700
and metrics and
making sure that we

00:28:10.700 --> 00:28:13.550
can have the most optimal
leadership performance, which

00:28:13.550 --> 00:28:16.280
is why we can change
the split leader,

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:19.400
split group leader on
the fly all the time.

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:27.320
So now, we need to talk
about how these splits are

00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:29.110
assigned to the nodes.

00:28:29.110 --> 00:28:31.040
So if you remember
our previous picture,

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:34.970
we have nodes that are compute
units that can come and go,

00:28:34.970 --> 00:28:35.650
essentially.

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:38.420
And then there's storage,
which is the database

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:43.160
and how it's split across with
these splits across these keys.

00:28:43.160 --> 00:28:45.570
But then these splits
have to be operated upon,

00:28:45.570 --> 00:28:49.220
and so they're assigned
to nodes within a zone.

00:28:49.220 --> 00:28:53.660
And each zone assigns splits
to nodes independently based

00:28:53.660 --> 00:28:56.630
on the health metrics and
many other heuristics.

00:28:56.630 --> 00:28:58.380
Again, this is
done automatically,

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:00.920
so if a node goes
down, then the split

00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:04.010
is rebalanced to
a different node.

00:29:04.010 --> 00:29:06.170
Or if we just so
choose, because of load

00:29:06.170 --> 00:29:08.000
and other
characteristics, we just

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:10.950
move splits between
nodes all the time.

00:29:10.950 --> 00:29:14.000
So in this case, we're
saying that Node 1

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:17.090
from our three-node example
in the regional instance

00:29:17.090 --> 00:29:19.640
configuration that we had
talked about earlier, split 0

00:29:19.640 --> 00:29:20.730
goes to Node 1.

00:29:20.730 --> 00:29:24.620
Split 1 goes to Node 2, and
then split 2 and 3 go to Node 3.

00:29:24.620 --> 00:29:29.060
And if conditions are such that
we think one of these splits

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:31.760
is getting overloaded, then
either there'll be a new split

00:29:31.760 --> 00:29:34.120
and go to a different
node, or split 3

00:29:34.120 --> 00:29:35.600
itself can go to
a different node.

00:29:40.720 --> 00:29:44.380
So as I said, it's important to
understand that a key range is

00:29:44.380 --> 00:29:45.490
how we look at--

00:29:45.490 --> 00:29:49.810
Spanner looks at the data in
the database as a key range.

00:29:49.810 --> 00:29:54.250
And we basically split
it across that key range.

00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:56.110
So as I said, we could
have multiple splits.

00:29:56.110 --> 00:29:58.970
We don't even have to have
splits matching anything else,

00:29:58.970 --> 00:30:00.190
but it's not like if
you have three nodes,

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:01.030
we'll have three splits.

00:30:01.030 --> 00:30:02.405
We actually have
multiple splits,

00:30:02.405 --> 00:30:05.830
and the splits can be merged
and extracted out as well.

00:30:10.970 --> 00:30:16.450
So because we look at the entire
database as a set of keys,

00:30:16.450 --> 00:30:19.850
as a key range, choice
of key is important.

00:30:19.850 --> 00:30:23.020
And this is true for basically
all relational databases,

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:27.640
because the relationships are
primarily defined through keys.

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:31.070
The key choice can dramatically
affect performance.

00:30:31.070 --> 00:30:35.110
For example, if you're
wanting to query

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:38.590
a set of keys or a set
of data that is not

00:30:38.590 --> 00:30:41.110
in primary key order, then
you have to create an index,

00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:44.680
where the index keys are
in the order in which you

00:30:44.680 --> 00:30:48.190
expect to be querying them
for faster response and faster

00:30:48.190 --> 00:30:50.650
scans.

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:54.910
In general-- and this is,
again, true for most databases.

00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:57.490
It's recommended to not
use sequential numbers

00:30:57.490 --> 00:31:01.360
or timestamps as keys, because
again, we are splitting up keys

00:31:01.360 --> 00:31:05.176
and then sharding the
data across the key range

00:31:05.176 --> 00:31:06.550
in those splits,
and then putting

00:31:06.550 --> 00:31:08.540
the splits on multiple nodes.

00:31:08.540 --> 00:31:10.180
So if we have to--

00:31:10.180 --> 00:31:14.590
if you're sequentially
adding numbers or timestamps

00:31:14.590 --> 00:31:17.230
to your key range, then
that means everything

00:31:17.230 --> 00:31:20.080
in a particular split in
a particular key range

00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:21.370
is being affected.

00:31:21.370 --> 00:31:24.730
And that entire split is
being served by a node,

00:31:24.730 --> 00:31:28.480
so therefore, it
becomes a hotspot.

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:30.740
And again, for most
relational databases,

00:31:30.740 --> 00:31:33.770
this is a common anti-pattern.

00:31:33.770 --> 00:31:37.090
But especially for Spanner,
it's definitely not recommended

00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:39.910
that you choose your
ascending and descending

00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:41.970
sequential numbers
or timestamps.

00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:44.320
So if you need to
do that, then we

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:46.810
recommend making it second,
third, or fourth part

00:31:46.810 --> 00:31:48.610
of a composite key,
because the Spanner can

00:31:48.610 --> 00:31:49.870
take composite keys.

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:54.190
So you can actually put these
ascending and descending

00:31:54.190 --> 00:31:57.670
numbers as a set, as a non-first
part of the primary key

00:31:57.670 --> 00:32:00.560
so that we can split across
those and shard the data.

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:03.760
So basically, when you're
designing your schema,

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:05.590
you want to think
about how it can

00:32:05.590 --> 00:32:07.720
be sharded and
parallelized, because that's

00:32:07.720 --> 00:32:11.560
the power of Spanner is to be
able to shard and parallelize--

00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:15.970
shard data and parallelize
operations on that data

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:16.990
automatically.

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:19.540
So if you allow the system
to split across keys,

00:32:19.540 --> 00:32:23.402
then it can be better at
parallelizing the workload,

00:32:23.402 --> 00:32:25.360
thereby giving you better
throughput and better

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:26.480
performance.

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:30.490
And there are more information
on this on our documentation

00:32:30.490 --> 00:32:33.790
page on cloud.google.com
/spanner/docs/bestpractices.

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:39.480
So life of a strong read--

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:44.490
so if you're trying to get
the latest data, what happens

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:51.210
is your request goes to the
closest replica to you, which

00:32:51.210 --> 00:32:53.340
will look up the
split information,

00:32:53.340 --> 00:32:56.340
and then will ask
the leader, what

00:32:56.340 --> 00:33:00.210
is the latest
commit on that data?

00:33:00.210 --> 00:33:03.360
And then it gets a response
back on what the latest commit

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:08.910
timestamp was, and then it
will respond with the data

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:10.110
at that timestamp.

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:13.500
So again, to dig
deeper, if we say,

00:33:13.500 --> 00:33:19.370
read from my table
key 123, then the user

00:33:19.370 --> 00:33:21.470
makes that request that
gets put on our Google

00:33:21.470 --> 00:33:24.410
network, Google front end or
the Cloud Spanner front end,

00:33:24.410 --> 00:33:28.310
and then goes to the zone
that is closest to you that

00:33:28.310 --> 00:33:30.270
can serve that information.

00:33:30.270 --> 00:33:33.220
And in this case, because
it's a strong read

00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:36.110
as in the latest data written--

00:33:36.110 --> 00:33:37.730
the request is for that--

00:33:37.730 --> 00:33:39.600
we have to, as I had
mentioned earlier,

00:33:39.600 --> 00:33:41.240
we have to send a
ping to the leader

00:33:41.240 --> 00:33:45.410
to say, OK, what was the
last time at which data

00:33:45.410 --> 00:33:47.780
was committed for this key?

00:33:47.780 --> 00:33:53.840
And with that information, it
can serve the data from locally

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:55.800
to the user.

00:33:55.800 --> 00:33:58.190
Now, the obvious
question here is,

00:33:58.190 --> 00:34:00.925
what happens if the
data hasn't been sent?

00:34:00.925 --> 00:34:02.300
In this case, the
mutation hasn't

00:34:02.300 --> 00:34:06.050
been sent to the
non-leader splits, which

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:07.910
is a great question.

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:11.389
What happens is, as I said
earlier, as part of the commit,

00:34:11.389 --> 00:34:13.280
we're actually sending
out the mutations

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:15.020
to all the other replicas.

00:34:15.020 --> 00:34:17.420
So chances are that
it's already been

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:21.260
received by split 2,
zone A in this case,

00:34:21.260 --> 00:34:29.000
and will be served by the
zone A split to the requester.

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:32.600
If it is the case that
the zone A, split 2

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:36.560
hasn't received
data at time T bang

00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:39.710
is mentioned here, which is
the latest timestamp of commit,

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:42.380
then there might be a
little bit of a wait

00:34:42.380 --> 00:34:44.330
to get to that
timestamp, and then

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:46.639
get the data of that timestamp,
and then respond back.

00:34:46.639 --> 00:34:48.430
But as I said, in most
cases, because we're

00:34:48.430 --> 00:34:52.310
sending the data out
parallely, there shouldn't be--

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:56.474
these latencies are generally
hidden within the commit time

00:34:56.474 --> 00:34:56.974
itself.

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:04.160
In terms of a
boundedly stale read,

00:35:04.160 --> 00:35:05.780
as I talked about
earlier, if you

00:35:05.780 --> 00:35:11.960
make a request for this key,
123 again, but with a maximum

00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:18.080
15-second staleness, what that
means is the local replica

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:21.980
is confident that it has
the data at that time.

00:35:21.980 --> 00:35:23.600
The older you go,
the more chances

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:26.180
are that the mutations
have been replicated

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:28.910
to the other
replicas, which means

00:35:28.910 --> 00:35:32.712
that it can serve this
request without even

00:35:32.712 --> 00:35:33.920
having to talk to the leader.

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:41.700
So in terms of
single row writes,

00:35:41.700 --> 00:35:43.770
so basically, what
this means is that we

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:48.750
are trying to commit data to
a single key or a single row

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:50.834
in this configuration.

00:35:50.834 --> 00:35:52.750
In this case, it's a
three-zone configuration,

00:35:52.750 --> 00:35:57.990
but the same applies to a
multi-reading configuration

00:35:57.990 --> 00:35:59.020
as well.

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:02.310
So basically, the request-- it
makes a write request, and then

00:36:02.310 --> 00:36:04.590
the write request goes
directly to the leader.

00:36:04.590 --> 00:36:06.750
And again, the
API and the system

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:09.210
itself know where the leader
is, so the write request

00:36:09.210 --> 00:36:12.000
is directly routed
to the leader.

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:13.680
The leader acquires locks.

00:36:13.680 --> 00:36:15.450
That's the whole
point of leadership

00:36:15.450 --> 00:36:22.590
is to be the sole proprietor
of committing data to those--

00:36:22.590 --> 00:36:24.740
committing changes to that
data, and therefore, it

00:36:24.740 --> 00:36:25.380
has the locks.

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:27.180
So it'll acquire the
locks, and then it

00:36:27.180 --> 00:36:30.210
will send the write request
to the other replicas

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:32.820
within the form-- in this
case, the two other replicas

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:35.120
in this configuration.

00:36:35.120 --> 00:36:37.384
And the replicas
will basically acq--

00:36:37.384 --> 00:36:39.800
so it's basically two-faced
commit where the replicas will

00:36:39.800 --> 00:36:41.174
acqs that they're
ready to write,

00:36:41.174 --> 00:36:43.470
that they agree
with this proposal.

00:36:43.470 --> 00:36:48.230
And once the split leaders,
the two leader in zone B

00:36:48.230 --> 00:36:52.340
gets acqs back from a
majority, so basically,

00:36:52.340 --> 00:36:55.280
from one other zone in this
case, two out of three or three

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:59.810
out of five, it'll
release the logs,

00:36:59.810 --> 00:37:03.110
and it'll apply the
changes locally, and then

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:08.630
send a successor request
back to the requester.

00:37:08.630 --> 00:37:10.730
So again, as I was
saying earlier,

00:37:10.730 --> 00:37:12.680
as part of the commit
protocol, the writes

00:37:12.680 --> 00:37:14.805
are actually mutations are
actually being sent out,

00:37:14.805 --> 00:37:16.263
which means that
you don't actually

00:37:16.263 --> 00:37:18.680
have to wait for the data
to be applied at the leader,

00:37:18.680 --> 00:37:22.929
and then have the data be
replicated to other replicas.

00:37:22.929 --> 00:37:25.220
The mutations are being
replicated as part of a commit,

00:37:25.220 --> 00:37:27.440
and therefore, they're
being applied independently.

00:37:27.440 --> 00:37:30.680
And chances are that if
you read the same data

00:37:30.680 --> 00:37:33.350
after the commit
responses come back,

00:37:33.350 --> 00:37:36.680
that you will get
the data immediately

00:37:36.680 --> 00:37:39.277
from the local replica.

00:37:39.277 --> 00:37:40.860
Let's look at a more
interesting case,

00:37:40.860 --> 00:37:44.070
which is writing across
two different splits.

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:46.920
So if you want to write across--

00:37:46.920 --> 00:37:49.500
in a table or
multiple tables, which

00:37:49.500 --> 00:37:52.140
happen to be on two
different splits,

00:37:52.140 --> 00:37:54.240
then those splits will
have to coordinate in order

00:37:54.240 --> 00:37:56.406
to commit the write.

00:37:56.406 --> 00:37:57.780
So let's look at
an example where

00:37:57.780 --> 00:38:01.620
we are going to commit data,
such that split 1 and split 2

00:38:01.620 --> 00:38:02.250
are involved.

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:04.930
And as you can see
from this diagram,

00:38:04.930 --> 00:38:09.720
split 1 has leader in
zone C, and split 2

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:13.120
has leader in zone B.

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:20.320
So when a write request comes in
to commit data to row X and Y,

00:38:20.320 --> 00:38:23.290
as part of that write
request, the coordinator

00:38:23.290 --> 00:38:25.840
is already chosen
by the client, which

00:38:25.840 --> 00:38:28.640
means chosen by the API itself.

00:38:28.640 --> 00:38:31.720
In this case, we have
chosen zone C leader

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:34.150
to be the coordinator.

00:38:34.150 --> 00:38:38.240
So each of these splits
will acquire the locks

00:38:38.240 --> 00:38:41.900
for making changes
to row X and y.

00:38:41.900 --> 00:38:47.510
And then they will independently
talk to their replica

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:50.120
across the multiple zones.

00:38:50.120 --> 00:38:53.900
So at this point, similar to
the previous single row case,

00:38:53.900 --> 00:39:01.250
basically, they replicate the
data across the two other zones

00:39:01.250 --> 00:39:03.390
for their split.

00:39:03.390 --> 00:39:06.300
And then once they have
received acknowledgments

00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:09.960
back from those two other
replicated split groups

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:12.990
from their group,
split 2 is going

00:39:12.990 --> 00:39:15.450
to respond back to
the coordinator, which

00:39:15.450 --> 00:39:20.850
is the split 1 in zone C, to say
that they are ready to commit.

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:23.800
At that point, because
split C is a coordinator,

00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:27.300
it'll basically
tell split 2 to do

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:31.380
the commit of the
data, at which point

00:39:31.380 --> 00:39:35.730
each group leader will then
tell its other replicas to apply

00:39:35.730 --> 00:39:37.080
the change.

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:41.880
And the success is returned
back to the client.

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:43.849
So in this case,
the same protocol

00:39:43.849 --> 00:39:45.890
that we were talking about
in the single row case

00:39:45.890 --> 00:39:50.210
happens at each split level,
where each split leader informs

00:39:50.210 --> 00:39:54.560
its other replicas
to apply the change

00:39:54.560 --> 00:39:57.020
and to make the
proposal for the change.

00:39:57.020 --> 00:39:59.810
But then there's coordination
among split leaders

00:39:59.810 --> 00:40:02.540
on doing the commit
for all the rows

00:40:02.540 --> 00:40:04.060
that are part of this commit.

00:40:06.580 --> 00:40:09.280
So I know we covered
a lot of material,

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:12.460
but hopefully, it gave you
a sneak peek at, or at least

00:40:12.460 --> 00:40:15.790
a better understanding at
how Cloud Spanner works

00:40:15.790 --> 00:40:21.310
under the covers to get
external global consistency.

00:40:21.310 --> 00:40:24.040
Stay tuned for a live Q&amp;A. We'll
be back in less than a minute.

00:42:10.450 --> 00:42:11.680
Welcome back.

00:42:11.680 --> 00:42:16.750
We have a few questions that
we can go over with regard

00:42:16.750 --> 00:42:18.020
to what we just discussed.

00:42:18.020 --> 00:42:19.760
So let's get to them.

00:42:19.760 --> 00:42:23.380
So first question, "How does
Spanner know which kind of read

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:26.320
the client is asking for,
and should this functionality

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:28.010
be built into the client?"

00:42:28.010 --> 00:42:30.220
So yes, the question
was answered.

00:42:30.220 --> 00:42:32.020
Basically, the
functionality is actually

00:42:32.020 --> 00:42:36.610
built into the API,
where the API knows

00:42:36.610 --> 00:42:38.450
where to route the read.

00:42:38.450 --> 00:42:42.310
Also, the API itself,
when you make a request,

00:42:42.310 --> 00:42:45.170
you can actually-- when you are
specifying bounded staleness,

00:42:45.170 --> 00:42:46.450
it's part of the call.

00:42:46.450 --> 00:42:48.040
Part of the request
that you make

00:42:48.040 --> 00:42:50.440
is to say that I can
tolerate bounded staleness up

00:42:50.440 --> 00:42:54.670
to, let's say, 15 seconds
or 10 minutes or whatever.

00:42:54.670 --> 00:42:58.480
And because of that, it is
a pretty big hint to the API

00:42:58.480 --> 00:43:01.810
that it should use the bounded
stale read request instead

00:43:01.810 --> 00:43:02.800
of a strong read.

00:43:02.800 --> 00:43:05.770
If you specify nothing
and just say, give me

00:43:05.770 --> 00:43:10.600
key 1, value 1, then by default,
we give you the strong reads,

00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:13.390
because we expect all
databases, relational databases,

00:43:13.390 --> 00:43:15.970
we expect you to want
the consistent read

00:43:15.970 --> 00:43:20.460
answer, the most freshest
read that just happened.

00:43:20.460 --> 00:43:22.570
So hopefully, that answers that.

00:43:22.570 --> 00:43:27.490
"Any plans to have a standalone
simulator to test Spanner?"

00:43:27.490 --> 00:43:29.470
Yes, we have a plan for that.

00:43:29.470 --> 00:43:37.210
Basically, we're working on
open sourcing our SQL engine

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:40.780
and the relevant parts
of it that will then

00:43:40.780 --> 00:43:43.630
allow us to build a
simulator [AUDIO OUT]

00:43:43.630 --> 00:43:46.552
be used locally on
machines and stuff.

00:43:46.552 --> 00:43:49.010
And you don't have to constantly
be connected to the cloud.

00:43:49.010 --> 00:43:51.343
And machines and stuff, and
you don't have to constantly

00:43:51.343 --> 00:43:52.780
be connected to the cloud.

00:43:52.780 --> 00:43:55.180
We're working on it,
and hopefully, we'll

00:43:55.180 --> 00:43:58.810
have it soon enough.

00:43:58.810 --> 00:44:01.480
Next question, "Does
Spanner support

00:44:01.480 --> 00:44:04.000
column constraints such as
foreign key constraints?"

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:07.330
So so far, Spanner supports
foreign key constraints

00:44:07.330 --> 00:44:10.150
in the form of
interleaved tables.

00:44:10.150 --> 00:44:13.660
So basically, if you interleave
a table into a parent,

00:44:13.660 --> 00:44:17.200
then that automatically gives
it a foreign-- a key constraint,

00:44:17.200 --> 00:44:19.450
where you have to have
the parent in order

00:44:19.450 --> 00:44:21.047
to have the child.

00:44:21.047 --> 00:44:23.380
Well, beyond that, we don't
have foreign key constraints

00:44:23.380 --> 00:44:26.110
in the product yet.

00:44:26.110 --> 00:44:28.150
Hopefully-- we have
it in our backload,

00:44:28.150 --> 00:44:30.680
and hopefully, we'll have
it sometime in the future.

00:44:30.680 --> 00:44:33.940
So that's all the time we
had for questions today.

00:44:33.940 --> 00:44:35.740
Hopefully, you enjoyed
the talk, and stay

00:44:35.740 --> 00:44:40.690
tuned for talks coming every
Tuesday from Cloud OnAir

00:44:40.690 --> 00:44:42.640
webinars from Google Cloud.

00:44:42.640 --> 00:44:44.940
Thank you, and have a good day.

